TEAM does not bundle all of Medicare. It holds mandated hospitals accountable for five surgical episode categories across a 30-day post-discharge window: lower extremity joint replacement (LEJR), surgical hip/femur fracture treatment (SHFFT), spinal fusion, coronary artery bypass graft (CABG), and major bowel procedures.
If your service line is not on that list, TEAM may still touch you indirectly (shared PAC capacity, enterprise analytics), but the reconciliation math attaches to these five. For the legal definition of an episode, see What is an episode of care?
&lt;BlogFastFacts label={“Fast Facts — Five episodes”} items={[“LEJR — highest national volume (~204k TEAM-hospital episodes baseline across all five”,“LEJR majority share)”,“SHFFT — geriatric, frail, high PAC and delirium risk”,“Spinal fusion — device + rehab variability; outlier risk”,“CABG — lower count, high spend, cardiac rehab critical”,“Major bowel — infection and readmission sensitive”,“Window — discharge + 30 days (not 90-day BPCI-style for all historical models)”]} /&gt;

CMS TEAM pays — and claws back — on the full 30-day episode, not the index admission alone. Systems that already control (or tightly contract) post-acute care, employ the surgeons who generate episodes, and share a single EHR for discharge visibility start with structural advantages documented in earlier bundled-payment literature (Dummit et al., 2016; Finkelstein et al., 2018).
Brand reputation and academic status help quality scores; they do not automatically lower SNF length of stay. The networks below appear frequently on the March 2026 TEAM roster by hospital-name matching in Rainfall’s directory. Counts are approximate (naming is inconsistent across CCNs) and meant for strategic prioritization, not securities disclosure.
